---
intent: >
  Drop `brainPath` at every layer of provisionBrain (orchestrator, every step
  builder, the bundled `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper). The bundled
  template emits YAML null at the path slot of `mcpServerConfig.args` — no
  literal-string sentinel anywhere in the chain. The brain root path
  reaches dependent steps by reading the on-disk brain.aide via
  parseBrainAide; when the path slot reports null the brain root is unknown
  and the dependent steps reflect that. Drop legacy `obsidian`-keyed
  migration logic from the MCP step entirely — only the four post-rework
  branches remain (cold install, brain present + matches, brain present +
  drift, malformed JSON). Update the test suites to remove every test
  exercising `brainPath` provided/absent paths, every test asserting
  `<BRAIN_PATH>` placeholder, and every test for legacy obsidian-key
  migration; add tests for YAML-null emission, null propagation through
  the MCP prescription, and seven-step idempotency under the new contract.
---

## Project Structure

The plan governs the `provisionBrain/` module only. Every file in the tree
below already exists; this plan modifies files in place — no files are
added or deleted. The module's directory layout is fixed by the
orchestrator/helper convention (every helper in its own subfolder); this
plan does not introduce new helper subfolders because `buildUpdatePlaybookStep`
already lives inline as a sibling of the other four artifact-step builders
in `index.ts` (the prior cycle established that ordering and the strategy
section here keeps it).

```
provisionBrain/
├── .aide                                        ← intent spec (existing — rewritten Stage 1+3, not touched here)
├── plan.aide                                    ← this plan (rewritten this cycle)
├── todo.aide                                    ← from prior cycle (informational; not touched by this plan)
├── index.ts                                     ← orchestrator (existing — modified)
│   ├── provisionBrain(projectRoot: string, mcpConfigPath: string): Promise<InitStep[]>
│   │     — orchestrates the seven InitSteps in fixed order; signature drops
│   │       brainPath
│   ├── buildBrainAideStep(projectRoot: string): Promise<InitStep>
│   │     — invokes obsidianBrainAideTemplate() with no arguments; emits
│   │       seed-semantic InitStep
│   ├── resolveBrainRoot(projectRoot, brainAideStep): Promise<string | null>
│   │     [NEW helper]
│   │     — single point that reads the scaffolded or on-disk brain.aide,
│   │       extracts mcpServerConfig.args[<path-slot>], returns the string when
│   │       the slot carries a real path or null when the slot carries YAML null
│   │       (or the parse fails — null collapses both unwired and unparseable
│   │       cases since the four dependent steps treat both the same: brain
│   │       root unknown). The path-slot index is read from a module-local
│   │       constant (PATH_SLOT_INDEX = 3) rather than introspected, matching
│   │       the spec's "package never introspects slot semantics" rule. The
│   │       constant's source of truth is the integration's body section in
│   │       brain.aide, copied verbatim into the bundled obsidian template;
│   │       the package code uses 3 because that is what the bundled template
│   │       emits, not because the package picked a slot semantic.
│   ├── buildBrainRootStep(brainRoot: string | null): Promise<InitStep>
│   │     — accepts the resolved brain root path or null; null routes to
│   │       a would-skip step naming the unwired brain configuration; a
│   │       string routes to the existing presence-or-create logic
│   ├── buildPlaybookStep(brainRoot: string | null, brainAideStep): Promise<InitStep>
│   │     — same null-aware gating as buildBrainRootStep; reads body.playbookIndex
│   ├── buildStudyPlaybookStep(brainRoot: string | null, brainAideStep): Promise<InitStep>
│   │     — same null-aware gating; reads body.studyPlaybook
│   ├── buildUpdatePlaybookStep(brainRoot: string | null, brainAideStep): Promise<InitStep>
│   │     — same null-aware gating; reads body.updatePlaybook
│   ├── buildResearchStep(brainRoot: string | null, brainAideStep): Promise<InitStep>
│   │     — same null-aware gating; reads body.researchIndex
│   ├── buildBrainMcpStep(projectRoot, brainAideStep, mcpConfigPath): Promise<InitStep>
│   │     — only four branches survive: cold install (no `brain` key) →
│   │       would-create; brain key present + entry matches → exists;
│   │       brain key present + entry differs → would-overwrite; malformed
│   │       JSON → would-create with configMalformed: true. The
│   │       hasObsidian inspection, the legacy-only branch, and the
│   │       both-keys transitional branch are all removed. Args carrying
│   │       null entries propagate verbatim through the prescription
│   │       (no defensive substitution).
│   ├── resolveBrainAideBody(projectRoot, brainAideStep):
│   │     Promise<{ playbookIndex: string; studyPlaybook: string;
│   │                updatePlaybook: string; researchIndex: string } | null>
│   │     — unchanged from prior cycle; destructures only the four install-time-
│   │       seed keys
│   ├── resolveBrainAideConfig(projectRoot, brainAideStep):
│   │     Promise<{ command: string; args: (string | null)[] } | null>
│   │     — return type widened: args is now (string | null)[]; behaviour
│   │       unchanged otherwise (delegates to interpolateArgs which already
│   │       passes null through under the broadened parser contract)
│   ├── exists(path: string): Promise<boolean>                                ← unchanged
│   ├── brainRootExists(brainPath: string): Promise<boolean>                  ← unchanged
│   ├── safeReadFile(path: string): Promise<string>                           ← unchanged
│   ├── BRAIN_ROOT_DIRS: readonly ["research", "process/retro", "coding-playbook"] ← unchanged
│   └── PATH_SLOT_INDEX: 3 (module-local constant)                            [NEW]
│         — index into mcpServerConfig.args at which the bundled obsidian
│           template lands the path. Used only by resolveBrainRoot; never
│           introspected, never branched on. Source of truth is the
│           integration's body section bytes in brain.aide; this constant is
│           a mirror of those bytes for the bundled obsidian default.
├── index.test.ts                                ← test suite (existing — modified)
│       — every test that calls provisionBrain(projectRoot, brainPath, mcpPath)
│         updated to the two-argument shape provisionBrain(projectRoot, mcpPath)
│       — every test asserting <BRAIN_PATH> in args or in step content removed
│       — every test asserting legacy obsidian-key migration behaviour removed
│         (the "5e: legacy obsidian-only .mcp.json" test, the
│         "transitional install (both brain and obsidian keys)" test)
│       — fixtures reading brain.aide from disk: the path slot in
│         mcpServerConfig.args is YAML null (a bare `-` line in YAML), and
│         tests using makeSixSectionBrainAide accept an args parameter that
│         may contain the literal value null
│       — new tests cover: (a) bundled template emits YAML null at args[3];
│         (b) cold install: derived MCP prescription carries null verbatim
│         at args[3]; (c) brain root path is read from on-disk brain.aide
│         when present (not from a parameter); (d) when brain.aide path slot
│         is null, dependent artifact steps return would-skip; (e) when
│         brain.aide path slot is a real string, all four artifact steps
│         flow as before; (f) seven-step idempotency holds under the new
│         contract for both unwired (path-slot null) and wired (path-slot
│         filled) brain.aide files
└── obsidianBrainAideTemplate/
    ├── index.ts                                 ← template helper (existing — modified)
    │     └── obsidianBrainAideTemplate(): string
    │           — signature drops the brainPath parameter entirely; called
    │             with no arguments
    │           — emits YAML null at args[3] (POSIX) or args[3] (win32; same
    │             slot index after the cmd /c npx prefix). YAML null is
    │             produced by emitting a bare `- ` line with no scalar after
    │             the dash; YAML 1.1/1.2 parses that to JS `null`
    │           — every other body byte unchanged from prior cycle (six
    │             marker-bounded sections in fixed order, four entry-point
    │             artifacts referenced in orientation, etc.)
    │           — config-section prose: the `<BRAIN_PATH>` literal-placeholder
    │             references in the existing config-section bytes describe an
    │             unwired-state SIGNAL the agent looks for. The new contract
    │             replaces those references with prose describing YAML null at
    │             args[3] as the unwired-state signal. The bytes between
    │             `<!-- aide-config-start -->` and `<!-- aide-config-end -->`
    │             are fully package-authored methodology under the
    │             user-owned-section-bytes carve-out, and revising that prose
    │             to match the new contract is in scope here.
    └── index.test.ts                            ← template helper test suite (existing — modified)
            — every test asserting `<BRAIN_PATH>` in args or in section bytes
              is removed or rewritten to assert YAML null and the new prose
            — every test calling obsidianBrainAideTemplate(brainPath) is
              updated to obsidianBrainAideTemplate() (no arguments)
            — new assertions cover: (a) helper signature takes no parameters;
              (b) parsed args[3] is exactly JS null on POSIX; (c) parsed
              args[3] is exactly JS null on win32; (d) the literal string
              `<BRAIN_PATH>` does not appear anywhere in the template
              output; (e) config-section bytes mention "YAML null" or
              "null" in lieu of the retired sentinel; (f) parser round-trip
              continues to succeed under the broadened (string | null)[]
              args contract
```

## Prerequisites

The following work must complete before this plan's steps run.

- `src/service/parseBrainAide` must export the broadened
  `mcpServerConfig.args: (string | null)[]` typed contract from
  `parseBrainAide` and `parseBrainAideFromString` (both ok-variant
  returns), and the `interpolateArgs` helper must accept the broadened
  args type and pass null entries through verbatim (every null in the
  input lands at the same index in the output, no `replace` call on
  null). The shared types in `src/types/index.ts`
  (`BrainAideMcpServerConfig.args`, `McpPrescription.entry.args`,
  `McpServerEntry.args`) are widened to `(string | null)[]` so the
  parser's typed result, the `interpolateArgs` return value, and the
  prescription shape all agree at compile time. This plan assumes all
  three are widened together — `provisionBrain` consumes
  `parseBrainAide`'s ok-variant directly and forwards the args array
  into `prescription.entry.args` without copying or coercion, so a
  type mismatch at any of these three boundaries would prevent this
  plan's edits from compiling.

- The shared `InitStep`-emitting contract in `src/types/index.ts`
  continues to support the four existing statuses
  (`would-create`, `would-overwrite`, `would-skip`, `exists`); no new
  status is needed. The `would-skip` status with an `instructions`
  field is the path used when the brain root is unknown
  (path-slot null) — same shape this module already uses for the
  parse-failure branch.

## Plan

### 1. Drop the `brainPath` parameter from `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` and emit YAML null at the path slot

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`,
`coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`

The bundled template is the single source of launcher bytes and all six
body section bytes. This step rewrites it to take no arguments and to
emit YAML null at the path slot. Two equally-valid YAML idioms parse to
JS `null`: a bare `- ` (dash + space + nothing) line, or a bare `-`
(dash + newline) line. Either is acceptable; pick the form whose
indentation lines up cleanly under the surrounding `args:` block in the
emitted bytes. Both Windows and POSIX branches lose the `'${pathArg}'`
quoted-string entry and gain the bare-dash null entry in the same args
position. The path-slot index that results is 3 — the same index
documented in the integration's body section bytes for the bundled
Obsidian default.

- [ ] 1a. Change the helper signature in
  `obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts` from
  `obsidianBrainAideTemplate(brainPath?: string): string` to
  `obsidianBrainAideTemplate(): string`. Remove the `pathArg` local
  variable, the `<BRAIN_PATH>` fallback expression, and every
  `${pathArg}` interpolation in the emitted YAML.

- [ ] 1b. Update the `mcpServerConfig` YAML emission in both
  platform branches so the args list ends with a bare-dash YAML null
  entry instead of a quoted path string. Concretely: each platform's
  `args` block now ends with a `    -` line (four spaces of indent
  matching the surrounding indent + dash + newline; no scalar after
  the dash). The win32 branch continues to emit `'/c'`, `'npx'`,
  `'@bitbonsai/mcpvault'` as the first three entries; the POSIX
  branch continues to emit `'@bitbonsai/mcpvault'` as the first
  entry. The path slot becomes the bare-dash null entry in the
  position previously held by the quoted `${pathArg}` entry.

- [ ] 1c. Rewrite the `<!-- aide-config-start -->` /
  `<!-- aide-config-end -->` body section bytes so the prose
  describes the unwired-slot signal as YAML null at `args[3]` rather
  than the literal `<BRAIN_PATH>` placeholder string. Every reference
  to the literal `<BRAIN_PATH>` token in the config-section prose is
  replaced with a description in terms of YAML null. The five-step
  Obsidian wiring flow keeps its identify→ask→edit→sync→restart
  shape; only the wording about how to detect the unwired state and
  what to write into the path slot changes. Source-of-truth note:
  the worked good-example in the spec's Strategy section shows what
  the new prose looks like (the Step 1 brain.aide block);
  paraphrase that prose into the bundled template's config section.

- [ ] 1d. Update the JSDoc on `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` to (i)
  document the no-argument signature; (ii) describe the path slot as
  carrying YAML null in every emission, with the integration's
  `config` body section telling the agent which arg index means what
  for the bundled Obsidian default (`args[3]`); (iii) drop every
  mention of the retired `<BRAIN_PATH>` literal-string placeholder
  including the "When `brainPath` is omitted" / "When `brainPath` is
  provided" branches; (iv) keep the marker-name and section-order
  documentation as-is.

- [ ] 1e. Verify by hand that the literal string `<BRAIN_PATH>` does
  not appear anywhere in the modified template helper's source — not
  in code, not in the body-section bytes, not in JSDoc. Use a quick
  text search across `obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts`. Any
  remaining occurrence is a bug.

### 2. Update the template helper's test suite for the no-argument signature and YAML-null path slot

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`,
`coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

Tests assert against the parser's typed-key values, not against
hand-rolled byte matches in the test file. The retired
`<BRAIN_PATH>` token must not appear in any assertion or fixture;
the path-slot null must be asserted as a strict JS `null`
(`expect(x).toBe(null)` or `expect(x).toBeNull()`), not as falsy or
empty string.

- [ ] 2a. Update the `getTemplate(brainPath?, mockedPlatform?)` test
  helper in `obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.test.ts` so it takes only
  `mockedPlatform?: "win32" | "posix"` (drop the `brainPath`
  parameter). Every call site that passes `"/foo/my-vault"`,
  `"C:\\Users\\test\\my-vault"`, or `undefined` for a brain path is
  rewritten to call `getTemplate()` or `getTemplate(undefined,
  "win32")` with no path argument.

- [ ] 2b. Remove the entire `path is inlined, no placeholder
  (brainPath supplied)` describe block — the contract no longer
  inlines a path at any layer.

- [ ] 2c. Remove the entire `optional brainPath — placeholder
  sentinel behaviour` describe block — the contract no longer has a
  placeholder sentinel.

- [ ] 2d. Remove the entire `Windows path round-trip (backslash
  regression)` describe block — there is no caller-supplied path to
  round-trip.

- [ ] 2e. Update `platform branching — Windows`: drop the second
  test (`args are [/c, npx, @bitbonsai/mcpvault, brainPath] on
  win32`) and replace it with a test asserting the args are
  `["/c", "npx", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", null]` after a parser pass
  on win32 — element-by-element equality or `toEqual` with the
  literal `null` at index 3. The `command is cmd on win32` test is
  unchanged.

- [ ] 2f. Update `platform branching — POSIX`: drop the second
  test (`args are [@bitbonsai/mcpvault, brainPath] on linux`) and
  replace it with a test asserting the args are
  `["@bitbonsai/mcpvault", null]` — `toEqual` with the literal
  `null` at the last index. The `command is npx on linux` test is
  unchanged.

- [ ] 2g. Update the `round-trip parses cleanly under the new
  schema` block: every test still passes `brainPath` to
  `getTemplate` — drop those arguments. The
  `parseBrainAideFromString` round-trip continues to assert
  `kind === "ok"`; assertions on `result.playbookIndex.length > 0`
  and `result.researchIndex.length > 0` remain unchanged.

- [ ] 2h. Update the `config section ships the Obsidian wiring
  flow` block: rename or rewrite the test currently called
  `config mentions the <BRAIN_PATH> placeholder (detecting the
  un-wired state)` so it asserts the new prose instead. The
  replacement assertion checks that `result.config` contains
  prose naming the unwired-slot signal as YAML null at args[3]
  (a substring like `"YAML null"` or `"null at"` — pick a load-
  bearing substring from the new prose). Also drop the
  assertion that the prose contains the literal token
  `"<BRAIN_PATH>"`.

- [ ] 2i. Add a new test block `path slot is YAML null in every
  emission`. Tests:
  - on POSIX: `parseBrainAideFromString(template).mcpServerConfig.args[1]`
    is exactly `null` (last index for the POSIX shape).
  - on win32: `parseBrainAideFromString(template).mcpServerConfig.args[3]`
    is exactly `null` (last index for the win32 shape).
  - the literal string `<BRAIN_PATH>` does not appear anywhere in
    the unparsed template output (`expect(content).not.toContain("<BRAIN_PATH>")`).
  - calling `interpolateArgs(parsed)` returns an array whose null
    entry is preserved at the same index (not coerced to empty
