### 3. Drop the `brainPath` parameter from `provisionBrain` and every step builder; introduce `resolveBrainRoot` and the null-aware artifact-step gating

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`,
`coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`

The orchestrator and its step builders move to a no-`brainPath`
shape in lockstep. The brain root path is read from the on-disk (or
in-memory scaffolded) brain.aide via parseBrainAide, surfaced as
`string | null`, and threaded through the brain-root-dependent step
builders. When the path is null, the dependent steps emit
`would-skip` with a fixed `instructions` string explaining that the
brain root is unwired; when the path is a string, the existing
presence-or-create logic applies unchanged.

- [ ] 3a. Change the `provisionBrain` export signature in
  `index.ts` from
  `provisionBrain(projectRoot: string, brainPath: string | undefined,
  mcpConfigPath: string)` to
  `provisionBrain(projectRoot: string, mcpConfigPath: string)`. Drop
  the `brainPath` parameter and the `effectiveBrainPath = brainPath ?? ""`
  computation. Update the function-level JSDoc to (i) document the
  two-argument signature, (ii) name the seven-step output in fixed
  order, (iii) describe the YAML-null contract (the bundled template
  emits null at args[3]; the null propagates verbatim through the
  derived MCP entry and into `prescription.entry.args`), (iv) drop
  every `<BRAIN_PATH>` reference, (v) drop every reference to legacy
  obsidian-key migration.

- [ ] 3b. Update `buildBrainAideStep` to accept only `projectRoot:
  string`. The body calls `obsidianBrainAideTemplate()` with no
  arguments. The seed-semantic idempotency rule is unchanged:
  presence at `.aide/config/brain.aide` returns `exists`; absence
  returns `would-create` with template bytes. The JSDoc loses every
  reference to the optional `brainPath` parameter and the
  `<BRAIN_PATH>` sentinel.

- [ ] 3c. Add a new helper `resolveBrainRoot(projectRoot: string,
  brainAideStep: InitStep): Promise<string | null>` between
  `resolveBrainAideConfig` and `buildBrainAideStep` in source order
  (function-ordering convention: helpers above the orchestrator at
  the top of the file; orchestrator at the bottom). Behaviour: the
  helper invokes the same in-memory-vs-on-disk dispatch as
  `resolveBrainAideBody` and `resolveBrainAideConfig` (use
  `parseBrainAideFromString(brainAideStep.content)` when
  `brainAideStep.status === "would-create"` and
  `brainAideStep.content !== undefined`; otherwise
  `parseBrainAide(projectRoot)`). On a non-ok parse result, return
  `null`. On `kind === "ok"`, read
  `parseResult.mcpServerConfig.args[PATH_SLOT_INDEX]` and return it
  as-is when it is a string, or `null` when it is `null`. The helper
  does not introspect the args list further, does not attempt to
  guess a path slot from frontmatter, and never substitutes a
  fallback. JSDoc notes that the path-slot index is a constant
  mirror of the integration's body-section claim about which arg
  index means what for the bundled Obsidian default; the package
  never introspects slot semantics.

- [ ] 3d. Add a module-local constant
  `const PATH_SLOT_INDEX = 3 as const` at the top of `index.ts`
  alongside `BRAIN_ROOT_DIRS`. JSDoc names the source of truth
  (the integration's `<!-- aide-config-* -->` body section) and
  states that the package never introspects which slot is which
  — the constant exists because the bundled obsidian template
  hard-codes the path at args[3], not because the package picked
  a slot semantic.

- [ ] 3e. Update `buildBrainRootStep`'s signature to accept
  `brainRoot: string | null`. New behaviour: when `brainRoot` is
  `null`, return a `would-skip` step with `name: "Brain root
  directories"`, `category: "brain"`, `filePath: ""`, and a fixed
  `instructions` string of the form `"Brain root path is unwired.
  Run /aide:brain config to fill the path slot in brain.aide."`
  When `brainRoot` is a string, call `brainRootExists(brainRoot)`
  and emit the existing `exists` / `would-create` steps unchanged.
  The internal `brainRootExists` helper still takes `string`; the
  null gating happens one layer up.

- [ ] 3f. Update `buildPlaybookStep`'s signature to accept
  `brainRoot: string | null` (rename the parameter from
  `brainPath` to `brainRoot` to match the new naming —
  storage-agnostic and aligned with `BRAIN_ROOT_DIRS`). New
  behaviour: when `brainRoot` is `null`, return a `would-skip`
  step with `name: "Playbook entry-point"`, `category: "brain"`,
  `filePath: ""`, and the same fixed `instructions` string as
  step 3e. When `brainRoot` is a string, the existing logic
  applies — presence check at `join(brainRoot, "coding-playbook",
  "coding-playbook.md")`, `resolveBrainAideBody` for content,
  the existing parse-failure → `would-skip` path. JSDoc updated
  to name `playbookIndex` and the new marker pair
  (`aide-playbook-index-*`); update the prose to mention the
  null-gating behaviour as the upstream contract.

- [ ] 3g. Update `buildStudyPlaybookStep`'s signature, body, and
  JSDoc to mirror 3f (parameter renamed to `brainRoot:
  string | null`; null routes to `would-skip`; string routes to
  the existing presence-or-create logic at
  `join(brainRoot, "coding-playbook", "study-playbook.md")`;
  reads `body.studyPlaybook`).

- [ ] 3h. Update `buildUpdatePlaybookStep`'s signature, body, and
  JSDoc to mirror 3f (parameter renamed; null routes to
  `would-skip`; string routes to the existing logic at
  `join(brainRoot, "coding-playbook", "update-playbook.md")`;
  reads `body.updatePlaybook`).

- [ ] 3i. Update `buildResearchStep`'s signature, body, and JSDoc
  to mirror 3f (parameter renamed; null routes to `would-skip`;
  string routes to the existing logic at
  `join(brainRoot, "research", "research.md")`; reads
  `body.researchIndex`).

- [ ] 3j. Update the orchestrator body in `provisionBrain` to (i)
  call `buildBrainAideStep(projectRoot)` first; (ii) call the new
  `resolveBrainRoot(projectRoot, brainAideStep)` to get
  `brainRoot: string | null`; (iii) thread `brainRoot` through
  `buildBrainRootStep`, `buildPlaybookStep`,
  `buildStudyPlaybookStep`, `buildUpdatePlaybookStep`, and
  `buildResearchStep`; (iv) call `buildBrainMcpStep(projectRoot,
  brainAideStep, mcpConfigPath)` last. The returned array length
  is exactly 7 in fixed order (brain config, brain root,
  playbook, study-playbook, update-playbook, research, MCP).
  Every comment-block above each builder call updates to drop
  the `brainPath ?? ""` rationale paragraph and to describe
  the null-aware gating instead.

- [ ] 3k. Update `resolveBrainAideConfig` to return
  `Promise<{ command: string; args: (string | null)[] } | null>`.
  The body change is a single type assertion or signature edit:
  the call to `interpolateArgs({ name, mcpServerConfig })` already
  returns `(string | null)[]` under the prerequisite-broadened
  contract; the return-type annotation just lifts. Update JSDoc
  to mention that args may carry null entries (forwarded verbatim
  from the on-disk or in-memory brain.aide).

### 4. Drop legacy `obsidian`-key migration logic from `buildBrainMcpStep`; only the four post-rework branches remain

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`,
`coding-playbook/foundations/anti-patterns`

The MCP step's six-state on-disk handling is collapsed to four. The
`hasObsidian` inspection, the legacy-only branch, and the both-keys
transitional branch are removed. The remaining branches are: cold
install (no `brain` key) → `would-create`; brain key present and
matches → `exists`; brain key present and differs → `would-overwrite`;
malformed JSON → `would-create` with `configMalformed: true`. Args
carrying null entries propagate verbatim into the prescription;
`buildBrainMcpStep` does not inspect or substitute the null.

