  present-artifact steps `exists`; the one missing-artifact step
  `would-create`; step 7 matches → `exists`.

- [ ] 6c. Run `rtk tsc` on the workspace and confirm no type errors
  introduced by this plan's edits (in `provisionBrain/`,
  `obsidianBrainAideTemplate/`, the test files, or in
  `provisionBrain`'s callers — note: `service/install/index.ts`
  will fail to compile here because it still passes three
  arguments; that compile error is the surface signal the
  follow-up plan needs to land. Capture the error message and
  report it to the user as a follow-up, do NOT fix it inline).

## Decisions

- **`brainPath` is removed entirely from every signature in this
  module — there is no two-argument-vs-three-argument compatibility
  shim, no rest-parameter trick, no overload set. The spec is
  explicit (Strategy: "Signature takes `projectRoot` and
  `mcpConfigPath` only", outcomes.undesired: "Carrying a
  `brainPath` parameter on the orchestrator signature, on any
  per-step builder, or on the bundled template helper").** Adding
  any compatibility shim would re-couple the install pipeline to a
  caller-supplied path and break the
  cli/init-is-brain-agnostic invariant on the next refactor. The
  one external caller (`service/install/index.ts`) is explicitly
  out of scope; its compile error is the surface signal that the
  follow-up plan needs to land.

- **`buildBrainRootStep` and the four artifact-step builders take
  `brainRoot: string | null` rather than each builder owning its
  own parse-and-resolve logic.** A single new helper
  (`resolveBrainRoot`) reads brain.aide once and returns the path
  slot's value (or null) to the orchestrator; the orchestrator
  threads that value into the five dependent builders. This
  preserves the
  one-parser-pass-per-orchestrator-invocation invariant (the
  builders share the same parsed `brainAideStep.content`),
  keeps each builder's body simple
  (a `if (brainRoot === null)` early return → would-skip, then the
  existing logic), and matches the
  `resolveBrainAideBody`/`resolveBrainAideConfig` helper pattern
  that already lives in the file. The alternative (each builder
  re-parses brain.aide for itself) would multiply parser calls
  and fan out the null-detection logic across five sites instead
  of one.

- **`PATH_SLOT_INDEX = 3` is a module-local constant whose source
  of truth is the integration's body-section bytes in brain.aide,
  copied into the bundled obsidian template.** The package never
  introspects which slot is which — it uses 3 because the bundled
  template emits null at args[3]. The constant lives in `index.ts`
  (not in a separate file or in the template helper) because
  it's a one-liner, used in exactly one helper
  (`resolveBrainRoot`), and the JSDoc above it documents the
  source-of-truth chain. Hoisting the constant out of the
  function body keeps `resolveBrainRoot`'s code free of magic
  numbers without inflating the module's surface area. (For a
  hypothetical second integration whose path slot lives at a
  different index, the constant would need to be revisited; that
  is acceptable because the spec's design is explicit that the
  bundled obsidian default is the only integration the package
  ships, and any future integration would land its own path-slot
  index in its own body section bytes — at which point this
  constant graduates into a dispatch axis the package owns. That
  refactor is not in this plan's scope.)

- **Null-aware gating in the artifact-step builders uses
  `would-skip` with an `instructions` string rather than a new
  status.** The existing `InitStepStatus` union already supports
  `would-skip` with `instructions`; that shape is exactly what
  `buildPlaybookStep` (and its three siblings) currently emit on
  parse failure. Reusing it for the path-slot-null case keeps the
  status vocabulary closed and lets the install service's apply
  layer keep its existing handling of `would-skip` (no write,
  surface the instructions to the user). A new status would be
  cosmetic — it does not unlock any apply-layer behaviour the
  existing one doesn't.

- **Legacy obsidian-key migration is removed in one pass, not
  gated behind a transitional read path.** The user's instructions
  are explicit: drop legacy migration entirely. There is no
  `obsidian`-to-`brain` rename heuristic, no warning log, no
  "we noticed you have an obsidian key" branch. The user's
  `.mcp.json` may carry an `obsidian` key indefinitely from a
  pre-rework install; that key is the user's, not this package's,
  and sync's narrow contract (write only `mcpServers.brain`)
  preserves it. If the user wants the orphan obsidian key gone
  they remove it themselves; the package no longer offers
  migration.

- **The `<!-- aide-config-start -->` body-section bytes get
  rewritten in this plan rather than deferred to a later
  follow-up.** The spec's user-owned-section-bytes carve-out
  permits the bundled obsidian-flavored default to ship
  package-authored prose inside the seed sections. The current
  config-section prose references the literal `<BRAIN_PATH>`
  placeholder as the unwired-state signal; under the new
  contract, that signal is YAML null. Leaving the prose
  unchanged would ship a bundled default whose `config` body
  section instructs the agent to look for a string sentinel
  that never lands, breaking the wiring flow on first run.
  Rewriting the prose in this plan keeps the bundled template's
  install-time and agent-runtime contracts coherent; the
  rewrite stays inside the same template helper file so the
  one-cycle-one-source-of-truth rule for these bytes holds.

- **Tests assert path-slot null with strict `null` equality
  (`expect(x).toBe(null)` or `toEqual` with literal `null`)
  rather than truthiness or empty-string equality.** The spec is
  explicit (outcomes.undesired: "Substituting any other shape
  for the YAML null at the path slot of `mcpServerConfig.args`
  — replacing null with an empty string, dropping the null
  entry from the array entirely, or converting it into a
  literal-string sentinel ... anywhere along the chain"). A
  truthiness check would silently pass if the path slot were
  coerced to empty string; a `null` strict-equality check
  catches that regression. The same applies to the
  `args[i] !== null` versus `Boolean(args[i])` distinction in
  the test expectations.

- **No new constants are introduced for the artifact paths
  (`coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`, etc.).** The
  individual artifact builders continue to inline the
  `join(brainRoot, "coding-playbook", "coding-playbook.md")`
  shape. Hoisting these to a const map would obscure the
  one-builder-one-artifact-path invariant and make the
  storage-shape contract less visible at the call site. The
  paths already appear once each in the file; that is the right
  number.

- **The malformed-body fallback in artifact-step builders
  (`would-skip` with a parse-failure `instructions`) is reused
  unchanged for the unwired-path-slot case** — both surface the
  same `would-skip` status with the same shape. The two
  failure modes (parse failure vs unwired path slot) carry
  distinct `instructions` strings so the user can tell them
  apart in the install summary, but the status discriminant is
  shared. This keeps the apply layer's `would-skip` handling
  identical regardless of upstream cause.

- **The `BRAIN_ROOT_DIRS` constant stays unchanged** — the
  three required directories
  (`research`, `process/retro`, `coding-playbook`) accommodate
  every entry-point artifact path in the bundled obsidian
  default. No change to the directory contract is implied by
  the YAML-null pivot.

- **Storage-agnostic identifier renaming (`brainPath` →
  `brainRoot`) lands inside the artifact-step builders in this
  plan** because the parameter rename happens at the same time
  the parameter type changes (`string` → `string | null`); a
  dual-rename keeps the change atomic per builder. The
  `brainRoot` name aligns with the existing `BRAIN_ROOT_DIRS`
  constant and the spec's "brain root" vocabulary; the
  retired `brainPath` name carried connector-specific
  baggage (path-on-the-filesystem-of-an-Obsidian-vault) that
  the new shape does not assume.

- **The bundled obsidian template helper folder name
  (`obsidianBrainAideTemplate/`) stays as-is.** The spec
  carves this folder name out as the only allowed
  storage-specific identifier — it describes bundled template
  content, not a code-level dispatch axis. Renaming it (e.g.
  to `defaultBrainAideTemplate/`) is its own refactor across
  the install service and is outside this plan's scope.
